<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Rites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Liji, Classic of Rites, Lijing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Rites, or Liji, is a collection of texts describing the social forms, administrative structures and ceremonial rites of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> as interpreted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rites-of-zhou">Rites of Zhou</a> and the Book of Etiquette and Rites it forms the Three Li, the ritual part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a> of the Confucian tradition. Its chapters are of uncertain origin and date, and a copy of its 'Black Robes' chapter was found in a Guodian tomb sealed around 300 BCE. Zheng Xuan's annotated edition of 49 books remains the standard text, and its chapters were the foundation of the Great Learning and the Doctrine of the Mean.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-rites.jpg" alt="Book of Rites" width="330" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annotated_Classic_of_Rites_59.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Book of Rites is a diverse compilation of texts of uncertain origin and date. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rites-of-zhou">Rites of Zhou</a> and the Book of Etiquette and Ceremonial, it has no consistent overall structure. Some sections define ritual terms, and others deal with the life and teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>. Parts have been traced to earlier works such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xunzi-philosopher">Xunzi</a> and the Lüshi Chunqiu, while other sections are believed to date from the Former Han period. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the Doubting antiquity movement questioned whether it contains genuine Warring States documents. In 1993 a copy of the Black Robes chapter was found in Tomb 1 of the Guodian tombs in Jingmen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>, sealed around 300 BCE, which reopened the debate over the dating of other chapters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Many Confucian texts were destroyed in the Burning of the Books in 213 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a>, but scholars who had memorised or hidden the classics helped to rebuild them in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. The Book of Rites was said to have been fully recovered, though the Classic of Music could not be recompiled and survives only in fragments, mainly in the Record of Music chapter of this book. According to the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>, Dai De cut a collection of 214 books to 85 in the 1st century BCE, and his nephew Dai Sheng abridged it to 46. Ma <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong</a> added three more. Later scholarship doubts that account.</p><p>Zheng Xuan, a student of Ma Rong, produced an annotated edition of 49 books that became the authoritative version and is still the standard one. It formed the basis of the Right Meaning of the Ritual Records, the imperially sanctioned text and commentary established in 653 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Chapters of the book provided the foundation for the Great Learning and the Doctrine of the Mean. In the Song dynasty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> edited these two texts and, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>, they became the Four Books. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a> and the Four Books as orthodox, and the Ming and Qing upheld the choice, they became the standard curriculum for the civil service examinations. From 1313 to 1905 mastery of them was essential for anyone seeking official posts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Book of Rites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Songs, a collection of ancient poems; the Book of Documents, speeches and records of early kings; the <b>Book of Rites</b>, on ceremony and conduct; the Book of Changes, the Yijing, a manual of divination with philosophical commentaries; and the Spring and Autumn Annals, a chronicle of the…</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The layout also followed ancient customs set down in the <b>Book of Rites</b>, with ancestral temples in front of the palace, storage in the front part of the complex and residences at the back.</blockquote><a href="/work/forbidden-city">Forbidden City · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
